    @tableofcontents

    @section python_1_3 python Module Version 1.3
    <b>Security:</b>
    - %Python code can no longer be run from %Qore Programs restricted with
      @ref Qore::PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS "PO_NO_UNCONTROLLED_APIS" (included in
      @ref Qore::PO_NO_EXTERNAL_ACCESS "PO_NO_EXTERNAL_ACCESS", @ref Qore::PO_NO_IO "PO_NO_IO" and
      @ref Qore::PO_LOCKDOWN "PO_LOCKDOWN"): the @ref Python::PythonProgram "PythonProgram" class is now in the
      \c UNCONTROLLED_API functional domain as well as in \c EMBEDDED_LOGIC, and the \c python module commands are
      rejected in such Programs (and \c parse also with @ref Qore::PO_NO_EMBEDDED_LOGIC "PO_NO_EMBEDDED_LOGIC"), also
      when the %Program inherits the module from its parent.  Previously such Programs could run any %Python code,
      which is not subject to %Qore's restrictions (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5506">issue
      5506</a>); see @ref python_restrictions

    <b>New Features and Enhancements:</b>
    - Added support for Python 3.12, 3.13, 3.14, 3.15, and 3.16 (tested with the first alpha release of 3.16)
    - Added support for the free-threading mode (PEP 703) of Python 3.14 and later
    - The minimum supported %Python version is now 3.11
    - Added support for interruptible I/O for graceful termination of sandboxed programs; interrupt checks are
      performed before acquiring the Python GIL when a sandbox manager is active
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5116">issue 5116</a>)
    - Added the @ref Python::PythonVersion "PythonVersion" constant providing the Python version string

    <b>Bug Fixes:</b>
    - absolute date/time values keep their instant between %Qore and %Python: the UTC offset of an aware
      \c datetime.datetime west of UTC was read without its days (-05:00 became +19:00), UTC was read as the current
      time zone, and %Qore values in other time zones were naive \c datetime.datetime values of their wall-clock time;
      a %Qore value in another time zone is now an aware \c datetime.datetime with its UTC offset.  Relative date/time
      values of more than about 24 days of hours no longer overflow, and \c datetime.timedelta values compare equal
      after a round trip (-1 second came back as -1 day + 86399 seconds).  A \c datetime.time is a %Qore date on
      1970-01-01 instead of a date in year 0, which could not be converted back
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5490">issue 5490</a>)
    - %Qore \c number values were converted to \c None in %Python, so %Python \c int values outside the 64-bit range
      did not survive a round trip: a number is now a %Python \c int (an exact integral value outside the 64-bit
      range) or a \c decimal.Decimal, and a \c decimal.Decimal is a %Qore \c number.  A %Qore value of a type that
      cannot be converted to %Python raises a \c PYTHON-VALUE-ERROR exception instead of becoming \c None, and a
      namespace constant that cannot be converted is skipped when a namespace is imported into %Python, as class
      constants are (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5489">issue 5489</a>)
    - %Python values that a %Qore value cannot represent raise an exception instead of crashing the process: a
      \c list or \c tuple that contains itself, a string or \c dict key with lone surrogates, and an \c int with more
      than 4300 digits (or more significant bits than a %Qore \c number can hold); a \c dict referenced more than
      once in a value is converted each time, where it was dropped or tripped an assertion, a \c dict that contains
      itself raises an exception instead of losing the key, and negative ints between -10^18 and the minimum 64-bit
      integer convert to \c int instead of \c number
      (<a href="https://github.com/qoretechnologies/qore/issues/5488">issue 5488</a>)
    - fixed crashes when a thread created by %Python (ex: \c threading.Thread) called a %Qore function or callable,
      constructed a %Qore object, read a member of a %Qore object, imported a %Qore class or released the last
      reference to a %Qore callable; such a thread is now registered with %Qore while it runs, so the %Qore objects
      it creates are kept and the %Qore resources it acquires (ex: a lock) stay its own from one call to the next, as
      in a %Qore thread, and it is deregistered when it ends
    - %Qore code called from a thread created by %Python (ex: a %Qore closure or a class from a %Qore module) runs
      under the @ref sandboxing "sandbox" of the %Program whose %Python code runs on the thread, as when that code is
      called on a %Qore thread; previously it ran with no sandbox
    - changes made to \c os.environ (and with \c os.putenv() and \c os.unsetenv()) are serialized with the
      environment functions of %Qore, which read and change the environment under one lock; previously %Python changed
      the environment while %Qore threads could read it, which could crash the process.  The environment variables that
      the module reads (\c PYTHONHOME, \c PYTHONPATH) and the environment that %Python copies into \c os.environ when
      it starts are read under the lock as well.
    - the copy of the environment that each %Python interpreter makes into \c os.environ when it is created, and the
      \c TZ variable that its \c time module reads when it is imported, are read under the environment lock of
      %Qore; previously a %Python interpreter created while a %Qore thread changed the
      environment could read it while it changed
    - a child process created with @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" while another thread holds a lock of the module can
      use %Python, as long as no other thread held the %Python GIL; previously the child could wait forever for a lock
      held by a thread that did not exist in it
    - \c os.fork() (and \c os.forkpty()) called in a %Python interpreter of the module (every interpreter of a
      @ref Python::PythonProgram "PythonProgram" or of a @ref Qore::Program "Program" using %Python, which are
      %Python subinterpreters) now raises a %Python \c RuntimeError; previously the child process died with "Fatal
      Python error: _PyInterpreterState_DeleteExceptMain: not main interpreter", as %Python supports \c fork() only in
      its main interpreter.  Processes can still be started (ex: with \c subprocess).
    - a child process created with @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" while other threads were using a %Python interpreter
      (ex: a %Qore thread in a call from %Python to %Qore, or a thread created by %Python) can delete the interpreter;
      previously the deletion waited forever for those threads, which do not exist in the child.  Such an interpreter
      is not finalized in the child if threads created by %Python in the parent had a state in it; it is left in
      memory.  New threads of the child that use an interpreter get their own %Python thread states.
    - @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" called from %Python code in a %Python process using %Qore through the \c qoreloader
      module runs %Python's fork protocol, as \c os.fork() does: the functions registered with
      \c os.register_at_fork() run, and the child no longer keeps the %Python state of the threads of the parent;
      previously the child's exit waited forever for the threads of the parent that are not daemon threads.  While
      %Python interpreters of %Qore exist in the process (@ref Python::PythonProgram "PythonProgram" objects, or
      @ref Qore::Program "Program" objects using %Python), the fork is made as before, since %Python's fork protocol
      crashes the child then (<a href="https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/139759">CPython issue 139759</a>,
      which \c os.fork() also has); see @ref python_fork
    - a child process created with @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" while another thread held the %Python GIL, which can
      never be released in the child, raises a \c PYTHON-FORK-ERROR exception when it calls %Python or creates a
      %Python interpreter, and exits normally; previously it waited forever for the GIL.  If the thread that called
      @ref Qore::fork() "fork()" was called from %Python, the child cannot return to %Python code: it writes an error
      message to \c stderr and exits with status 1.  This requires the internal C headers of %Python
      (\c internal/pycore_*.h, installed with the %Python headers) when the module is built.
    - a %Qore callable (closure or call reference) called from %Python runs without the %Python GIL, as %Qore
      functions and methods called from %Python do; previously a callable that waited for another %Python thread
      waited forever
    - fixed incomplete finalization of Python subinterpreters, including atexit hooks, free-threaded builds,
      and destruction on a different native thread
    - with %Python 3.12 and earlier, destroying a %Python interpreter in a thread that has the identifier of the
      thread that imported \c threading in it and has ended (thread identifiers are reused) no longer reports
      "Exception ignored in threading._shutdown" with an \c AssertionError, and now waits for the threads of the
      interpreter that are not daemon threads, which it did not do in this case
    - with %Python 3.13 and earlier, destroying a %Python interpreter (ex: a @ref Python::PythonProgram "PythonProgram"
      object, or a @ref Qore::Program "Program" using %Python) while a %Python daemon thread still runs in it no longer
      aborts the process with "Fatal Python error: Py_EndInterpreter: not the last thread": such a thread cannot be
      waited for, so the interpreter is left in memory with the thread running in it, and a call it makes to %Qore
      afterwards raises a %Python \c RuntimeError; with %Python 3.14, the interpreter is finalized and the thread exits
      when it next runs.  The interpreter still waits for the threads that are not daemon threads, as in %Python.
    - fixed a hang finalizing a Python subinterpreter with Python 3.12 and earlier on a native thread other than the
      thread that imported \c threading in it (ex: a @ref Qore::Program "Program" using %Python destroyed in another
      thread than the one that created it): the finalization waited for that thread's state, which was never deleted
    - fixed interpreter ownership of imported Qore wrappers when independent PythonProgram objects share a Qore program
    - preserve Qore proxies for Python objects passed between interpreters, preventing cross-interpreter garbage
      collection while preserving object identity
    - resolve import specifications and stack-inspection callables in their owning interpreter and propagate
      import-spec allocation failures without corrupting subsequent imports
    - fixed retained return values, constructed Qore objects, and Python references held by wrappers or callbacks after
      their interpreter is deleted
    - fixed heap-type reference counting and garbage collection of Qore wrapper instances
    - fixed retained traceback frames after converting Python exceptions to Qore exceptions
    - release generated wrapper metadata when hosted by native Python; retained Python functions and bound methods
      now own their metadata and report an error if called after Qore shutdown
    - fixed a crash importing %Qore classes into %Python when a class constant has no %Python representation;
      such constants (ex: \c java.time.Instant::MIN, whose year lies outside the range supported by
      \c datetime.datetime) are now skipped instead of terminating the process
    - fixed %Qore date/time values with a year outside the range of a 16-bit integer being silently converted to
      an unrelated date/time value; such values are now reported as errors
    - fixed error reporting when a %Qore value cannot be converted to a %Python value; conversion failures now
      always raise a %Qore exception

    <b>Build System Changes:</b>
    - Requires a C++17-compatible compiler

    @section python_1_2_1 python Module Version 1.2.1
    - fixed a thread-safety race condition in \c qpy_deregister() where the shared program set was accessed without
      proper locking
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/5048">issue 5048</a>)
    - fixed an incorrect assertion in \c getQoreDateTimeFromTime() that used \c PyDateTime_Check instead of checking
      for \c TimeType
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/5049">issue 5049</a>)
    - removed a debug \c printf() statement that was left in \c callPythonMethod()
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/5050">issue 5050</a>)
    - fixed a typo in an error message ("dictiomary" -> "dictionary")
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/5051">issue 5051</a>)

    @section python_1_2 python Module Version 1.2
    - enable proper Python stack trace reporting when exceptions are thrown in %Qore code called from %Python; %Python
      stack frames are now included in the %Qore stack trace
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4653">issue 4653</a>)

    @section python_1_1_7 python Module Version 1.1.7
    - enabled building with standard atomic operations for all platforms

    @section python_1_1_6 python Module Version 1.1.6
    - fixed a memory leak with subinterpreters when the module is initialized by python through the
      @ref python_qoreloader_moduleguide "qoreloader" module
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4652">issue 4652</a>)
    - fixed a crash on exit when %Qore and the python module were initialized from Java
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4651">issue 4651</a>)

    @section python_1_1_5 python Module Version 1.1.5
    - updated to support Python 3.11
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4650">issue 4650</a>)
    - fixed a crash related to Python subinterpreter cleanup when the module is loaded by native Python
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4649">issue 4649</a>)

    @section python_1_1_4 python Module Version 1.1.4
    - another fix handling subinterpreters
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4405">issue 4405</a>)

    @section python_1_1_3 python Module Version 1.1.3
    - @ref Python::PythonProgram::evalExpression() "PythonProgram::evalExpression()" not able to reference
      symbols in the program
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4406">issue 4406</a>)
    - subinterpreter errors using the \c python module from %Python
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4405">issue 4405</a>)

    @section python_1_1_2 python Module Version 1.1.2
    - not possible to use the \c qoreloader module from native %Python
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4398">issue 4398</a>)
    - not possible to call imported Java or %Qore static methods when a normal method exists in the same class with
      the same name
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4397">issue 4397</a>)

    @section python_1_1_1 python Module Version 1.1.1
    - mark %Qore namespaces imported from %Python modules so that other languages like Java can use consistent paths
      with %Python symbols
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4373">issue 4373</a>)

    @section python_1_1 python Module Version 1.1
    - added support for Python 3.10
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4334">issue 4334</a>)

    @section python_1_0_7 python Module Version 1.0.7
    - fixed a bug setting the %Qore program context in new %Python threads calling imported %Qore methods or functions
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4330">issue 4330</a>)

    @section python_1_0_6 python Module Version 1.0.6
    - fixed a bug handling exception propagation between %Qore and %Python
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4329">issue 4329</a>)

    @section python_1_0_5 python Module Version 1.0.5
    - fixed a deadlock in context switching when Python threads switch to %Qore or Java
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4328">issue 4328</a>)
    - fixed a crash when Python threads made Qore or Java calls
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4327">issue 4327</a>)
    - fixed a regression in recursion depth calculations
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4323">issue 4323</a>)

    @section python_1_0_4 python Module Version 1.0.4
    - fixed calculating the recursion depth in the initial thread so that more %Python modules can be loaded in single-
      threaded programs without changing the stack size
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4323">issue 4323</a>)
    - fixed errors import valid Python modules
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4322">issue 4322</a>)

    @section python_1_0_3 python Module Version 1.0.3
    - do not shut down the %Python library on exit, as it will cause a crash in exit handlers in %Python modules that
      require the library to be in place (ex: h5py module v 3.3.0)
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4290">issue 4290</a>)
    - fixed additional namespace clashes and namespace allocation issues for imported %Python classes in %Qore
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4289">issue 4289</a>)

    @section python_1_0_2 python Module Version 1.0.2
    - fixed issues handling the case when the python module is initialized with no Program context
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4153">issue 4153</a>)
    - fixed a bug handling Python constructors in classes derived from Qore or Java classes where the constructor code
      accesses Qore or Java class members or methods before initializing the class
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4141">issue 4141</a>)

    @section python_1_0_1 python Module Version 1.0.1
    - fixed a bug where abstract %Qore classes could be instantiated in %Python
      (<a href="https://github.com/qorelanguage/qore/issues/4044">issue 4044</a>)

    @section python_1_0 python Module Version 1.0
    - initial release
